{
  "id": "Compound::DB00284 - Gene::279",
  "verbalization": "Acarbose exerts its effects by binding to Amy1a and Amy1b, which are subunits of the alpha-amylase enzyme. This binding inhibits the enzymatic activity of alpha-amylase, thereby reducing the breakdown of carbohydrates in the digestive tract. The observed relationship between Acarbose and Amy2a is mediated through the alpha-amylase activity, as both Amy1a and Amy1b participate in the same enzymatic process. The evidence suggests that Acarbose indirectly influences Amy2a by modulating the activity of alpha-amylase, which is a key step in the digestion of starch. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust, mechanistic link between Acarbose and Amy2a, highlighting the importance of alpha-amylase in this relationship. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of alpha-amylase in the biological effects of Acarbose, providing a clear and concise explanation for the observed interaction.",
